---
// Phase 1.2 verification page. Lives at src/pages/dev/ (no underscore —
// see ADR-0014). Not a real site page; excluded from what ships via the
// deploy-time `rm -rf dist/dev` step (IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §10), not via
// Astro's routing. Delete once the real specimen page (Phase 1.7) supersedes it.
import '../../styles/fonts-test.css';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <style>
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 2rem;
        background: #fff;
      }
      .row {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        gap: 2rem;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
      }
      .sample {
        font-size: 24px;
        line-height: 1.5;
        /* A horizontal ruler every line-height so any mismatch between the
           two columns' cap-height/baseline is visible at a glance, without
           needing to measure anything by hand. */
        background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(
          to bottom,
          #eee 0,
          #eee 1px,
          transparent 1px,
          transparent 36px
        );
      }
      .label {
        font-family: monospace;
        font-size: 12px;
        color: #666;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      }
      /* Column A — the REAL font stack: real file, then its matched
         fallback, then a generic. In a normal network load this is what
         every real component uses. */
      .real {
        font-family: 'Source Serif 4 Var', 'Source Serif 4 Fallback', Georgia, serif;
      }
      /* Column B — the fallback face FORCED, bypassing the real font file
         entirely. This isolates whether the metric-override math from 1.2
         is correct, independent of network timing. If the overrides are
         right, this column's text sits on the same ruler lines as column A
         even though the letterforms (Georgia vs Source Serif) look different. */
      .forced-fallback {
        font-family: 'Source Serif 4 Fallback', serif;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1 style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;">
      Font metric check — compare row by row
    </h1>

    <div class="row">
      <div>
        <div class="label">A — real stack (Source Serif 4 Var)</div>
        <p class="sample real">
          The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. Größe, Öffnung, Übung — testing
          German glyphs too. 1234567890.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="label">B — fallback FORCED (Georgia, overridden)</div>
        <p class="sample forced-fallback">
          The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. Größe, Öffnung, Übung — testing
          German glyphs too. 1234567890.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 12px; color: #666; max-width: 60ch;">
      If 1.2's math is right, both paragraphs' text sits on the same background ruler
      lines and looks roughly the same SIZE, despite different letterforms. A visible size
      jump or baseline drift between A and B means an override value is off — check
      size-adjust first, it has the largest effect.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
